EPB standards shall include provisions to facilitate:

— the inclusion and promotion of innovative solutions;

— the inclusion of future needs;

— usability and the inclusion of added values and added societal benefits.

Common quality aspects comprise among others:

— verification and demonstration;

— relevance, sensitivity and balanced accuracy;

— applicability;

— transparency.

EPB standards with calculations shall provide a worked-out example of that calculation, using one or more common example cases.

NOTE 1 The aim is to analyse and evaluate the usability of the standards, which will make the quality of the standards explicit and provide the right mindset during the development.

The main aspects related to usability that shall be evaluated prior to publication of the EPB standard are:

— effort/cost;

— reproducibility;

— accuracy;

— consistency/transparency;

— sensitivity (to distinct better technologies).

NOTE 2 The example cases will help the standard writers to be aware of what problems occur in practice, give input for the standards and what the effect is for the overall accuracy, reproducibility and effort/cost.

At the same time, the necessity of including complexity or detail in the EPB standards can be explained to the national authorities more easily by illustrating the effect using the example cases.

The set-up of common example cases is specified in CEN/TS 16629.

To promote a clear overall insight in the results, also for cases where the calculation itself may have become quite complex, common reporting procedures (output) shall be developed:

— which key (calculation) results to be reported;

— at which different levels in the modular structure;

— how aggregated in space (building zones);

— how aggregated in time (time periods/ time steps);

— how aggregated according to services (heating, cooling, …);

— how aggregated by energy sector;

— …

— national and international policy level;

— national and international regulators and energy agencies;

— EPB assessors;

— manufacturers and component suppliers;

— building designers and system engineers;

— software developers;

— project developers and building companies;

— financial institutes;

— research and education.

It is not evident to keep the information accessible over the years, in particular because different organisations (ISO, CEN) and committees covering different domains are involved. Therefore EPB Center has been initiated, with support from the European Commission, to support the information and implementation of the set of EPB standards.

See CEN/TS 16629.

EPB standards should allow for adaptation for recently identified or future needs, such as.

— tightening the EP-requirements;

— include complementing aspects in the calculation; more specifically: comfort, CO2, health, indoor

environment quality (IEQ);

— addition of additional indicators (e.g. comfort indicators);

— incorporate new solutions (e.g. proven innovative solutions).

As stated in CEN/TS 16628, common quality aspects comprise among others:

— verification and demonstration;

— relevance, sensitivity and balanced accuracy;

— applicability;

— transparency.

This clause covers practical rules regarding layout and editing of the EPB standards, to ensure that EPB standards meet the overall quality, consistency and interoperability as indicated in the previous clauses. 14.2 Common template of an EPB standard

A common template for each EPB standard has been developed and applied since the first EPB standards (2009). The template has been and will be updated in the course of time due to new experiences and changing views, demands and conditions over the years.

The template shall contain information on at least:

— the structure of the standard, complementary to the structure required by CEN and/or ISO;

— the specification of the list of all output data;

— the specification of the list of all input data;

The common template shall contain technical and editorial rules for EPB standards, in addition to the CEN and ISO rules. This includes at least:

— quality aspects;

— preferred word order (e.g. a standard order for the technical systems);

— the way reference is made to other EPB standards;

— the way reference is made to European legislation or regulations (if any);

— ...

A common template for each EPB TR has been developed and applied since the first EPB standards (2009). The template has been and will be updated in the course of time due to new experiences and changing views, demands and conditions over the years.

The template shall contain information on at least:

— The structure of the report;

— If the accompanied standard contains calculation procedures: the reporting format of the example

case calculations and the reference to the spreadsheet location.

The additional technical and editorial rules for the TRs accompanying EPB standard shall be based on the additional rules for EPB standards, but adapted to accommodate the nature of this type of document. 14.5 Common template and technical and editorial rules for a spreadsheet

Each EPB standard containing calculation procedures shall be accompanied by a spreadsheet tool, to be developed in parallel with the standard. During the development of the standard example cases shall be calculated. When the draft standard goes into public enquiry, the spreadsheet with example cases is made available publicly available for demonstration and verification of the procedures. The example cases are described in the TR.

The spreadsheet is not part of the standard and shall be referenced only in the TR.

A common template for each spreadsheet has been developed and applied since the first EPB standards (2009). The template has been and will be updated in the course of time due to new experiences and changing views, demands and conditions over the years.

The common template shall contain information on:

— the standard that is covered;

— responsible organization or person responsible for its development;

— disclaimer;

